{"Sleep testing is hard for three reasons. First, patients wait one or two months for a sleep study. Second, putting on all the sensors often takes more than 40 minutes, and that makes people anxious. And third, they have to sleep in a lab, which is nothing like their own bed.":["0563e61a4b",16.73,17.94],"Imagine trying to fall asleep with more than twenty wires on your head and body, in a strange room. That's what a standard sleep test feels like. And you may wait a month or two just to get a bed.":["d74dba0221",10.65,12.18],"We need objective data to confirm sleep-disordered breathing, but PSG is a bottleneck. It needs 20 or more wires, the setup often takes more than 40 minutes, and patients wait one to two months.":["02a5bdab92",13.14,14.29],"PSG is still the gold standard, and we still need it. The problem is access. Patients wait one or two months, the setup takes more than 40 minutes, and they have to sleep in a very unnatural setting.":["8490d9c609",12.54,13.48],"Sleep tests are slow to get, take a long time to set up, and make people sleep in a very unnatural setting.":["acced63254",6.68,7.32],"Patients wait one or two months for a sleep test.":["2f402a9ebe",2.69,3.09],"Putting on the sensors often takes more than 40 minutes, with many wires.":["919732ab52",4.37,4.93],"Patients have to sleep in a very unnatural setting, and it makes them anxious.":["82dbf13032",4.42,4.86],"But we need objective data to confirm sleep-disordered breathing.":["3224498bab",3.73,4.22],"polysomnography":["51a5b3b549",1.39,1.58],"sleep-disordered breathing":["c7c52746b6",1.56,1.75],"objective":["205360f460",1.13,1.26],"hassle":["eeedeae221",0.94,0.96],"anxious":["98df500001",1.0,1.05],"Let me start with a simple question.":["1f30fced92",2.16,2.5],"On top of that":["eb9ea00d1d",1.15,1.52],"Besides":["3734107664",1.13,1.13],"And the worst part is":["fdd7ad9e6c",1.41,1.71],"That's why we need something simpler.":["0c5a026999",1.9,2.3],"PSG is the gold standard. Why not just use it?":["c33338fb9a",3.48,3.88],"wait one or two months":["612708878e",1.45,1.71],"It's a real hassle.":["9e5a260ce8",1.39,1.73],"Patients need to wait.":["e4b8ef2ad0",1.56,1.66],"Here's how it works. The radar sends out a signal, and it bounces back from the body. Every time the chest moves, even a little, the returning signal changes. From those changes, we rebuild the breathing wave, with no wires at all.":["a42e794c0e",14.55,15.47],"It's a bit like a bat using echoes. The device sends out a signal and listens for the echo. When your chest goes up and down, the echo changes slightly, and that tells us how you're breathing. Nothing touches you.":["8f6d073bc2",12.59,13.65],"It's a 24-GHz continuous-wave radar. We transmit from Tx and receive the reflection at Rx. Chest movement changes the time of flight and the phase of the received signal, and from those changes we reconstruct the breathing waveform.":["049b445a6f",16.3,17.37],"Because the radar doesn't need a big movement. Even a tiny shift of the chest changes the phase of the reflected signal. We track those small changes, breath by breath, and turn them into a breathing wave.":["d0c9b449f6",12.61,13.72],"The radar reads tiny chest movements from its reflected signal and turns them into a breathing wave, with no wires.":["65d13ed6f4",7.02,7.72],"The radar sends a signal and picks up the reflection.":["a5e59a7b3e",3.03,3.46],"When the chest moves a little, the received signal changes.":["9db2b0bcdc",3.39,3.69],"It changes the time of flight and the phase of the signal.":["832f3e64e8",3.29,3.84],"From those changes, we rebuild the breathing signal.":["0b102cd00d",3.03,3.33],"There are no wires, and nothing touches the patient.":["62f5b8786e",2.97,3.35],"reflection":["40f3468cd6",1.07,1.13],"time of flight":["566e5d22a1",1.13,1.37],"phase":["7ee7184c97",0.96,0.9],"24-gigahertz":["042e04c29a",1.45,1.71],"reconstruct":["17e53f7cd0",1.2,1.2],"Here's how it works.":["b3a79eb8ab",1.32,1.62],"When the chest moves":["7c50123a40",1.3,1.47],"From those changes, we":["f21282037f",1.56,1.75],"In other words":["2544e66186",1.13,1.37],"It's a bit like":["ec5db6f780",1.15,1.34],"How can a radar see something as small as breathing?":["514a726edb",2.69,3.05],"The radar sends a signal.":["cb02fe115e",1.69,1.96],"the signal bounces back":["938400c1a8",1.62,1.75],"breathing signal":["5f65fdba05",1.2,1.28],"Setting it up is simple. In the hospital, we mount the radar on the wall above the bed. At home, we use a floor stand that's easy to move. Either way, the radar sits about 75 to 90 centimeters away, and nothing touches the patient.":["28734865c8",14.93,16.64],"For you on the ward, there's nothing to attach. No wires, no patches. The radar is on a wall bracket above the bed, at about 75 to 90 centimeters. You don't have to fix sensors in the middle of the night.":["50e9798fe6",13.1,14.53],"You just sleep as usual. There's a small device on a stand next to your bed, or on the wall. It doesn't touch you, and you don't need to wear anything.":["0072f5d809",8.34,9.34],"It does matter. We set the radar at 75 to 90 centimeters. For fixed hospital beds, the wall bracket keeps it in place. At home, the floor stand is easy to move, so we just place it within that range.":["cb530817fa",13.01,14.57],"Put it on the wall or on a stand, 75 to 90 centimeters away, and the patient wears nothing.":["b6944cf950",6.06,6.96],"It goes on the wall above the bed, on an arm, or on a floor stand.":["93f5c40e49",3.95,4.4],"Wall bracket for fixed hospital beds; floor stand for homes.":["781e2bef21",3.82,4.22],"The radar is set at 75 to 90 centimeters.":["ef31ad765b",3.35,3.67],"Nothing touches the patient.":["d259c3b4fa",1.73,1.77],"bracket":["45b3afaeab",1.0,0.94],"wall-mounted":["25c26d5c8f",1.11,1.26],"floor stand":["2a78335451",1.15,1.3],"centimeters":["e6ec92b313",1.11,1.22],"Option A is":["049a3a144b",1.15,1.39],"Option B is":["9a2f0f5dd8",1.24,1.47],"It's best for":["2d05521d2a",1.2,1.32],"Either way":["6b33c74591",1.09,1.2],"The good thing is":["d299557e63",1.17,1.3],"Does the position of the radar have to be exact?":["12d7f57242",2.92,2.92],"mount the radar on the wall":["e8b1cd7a23",1.66,1.88],"75 to 90 centimeters":["d98e095800",2.22,2.45],"It's best for homes.":["862c369470",1.43,1.64],"at home":["585d4ab2ef",1.02,1.15],"Here's the catch. Sleep tests watch airflow, but radar watches the chest. During an obstructive event, the airway is blocked, so airflow stops. But the body keeps trying to breathe, so the chest still moves. If we judge apnea by how big the chest motion is, we get it wrong.":["caab51c2d8",17.15,18.45],"The scoring rules are defined on airflow: apnea is airflow stopping, hypopnea is airflow getting shallow. Our signal is chest motion, and its size depends on body size and sleep position. And during obstruction, the effort to breathe can increase. So size-based rules don't transfer. That was our biggest blind spot.":["808813561a",20.42,22.14],"Think of a blocked straw. You suck harder and harder, your cheeks move a lot, but no air comes through. The chest does the same thing when the airway closes. So the radar can see a lot of movement even when there's no air.":["fec5859d81",13.1,14.17],"Not always. In an obstructive event, the airway is blocked, so no air gets in. But the body struggles to breathe, so the chest moves again. A standard test sees no airflow, while the radar sees motion. That's exactly why chest motion alone can fool you.":["0f050f0da2",16.24,17.6],"Sleep tests watch airflow, the radar watches the chest, and during an obstruction the chest can move with no air.":["9bdbf7980a",6.81,7.7],"Sleep tests watch airflow, while radar watches the chest.":["4f0b54148e",3.52,3.86],"Every patient is different: body size and sleep position.":["ea2529555e",3.63,4.01],"During obstruction there is no airflow, but the chest keeps moving as the body struggles.":["1f33705013",5.18,5.59],"So judging apnea by the size of chest motion has a big error.":["7869b4f515",3.99,4.35],"airflow":["2528f15a18",1.02,1.07],"apnea":["c3c9e415fe",0.96,1.09],"hypopnea":["d2a7acb8e0",1.09,1.3],"obstructive":["5ecb19274b",1.15,1.24],"blind spot":["29adc4d0c5",1.15,1.34],"Here's the catch.":["7bce79d35c",1.2,1.37],"The tricky part is":["f374235cb5",1.3,1.47],"At first":["923c119a40",1.05,1.13],"That's why":["91cf861e08",1.05,1.13],"If the chest is moving, isn't the patient breathing?":["2a41299c06",2.99,3.35],"the chest still moves":["2e3d3d6d7b",1.41,1.52],"the biggest blind spot":["38717d29d4",1.56,1.71],"Because the airway is blocked, the airflow stops.":["95b02132fd",2.84,3.03],"Instead of measuring how big the chest motion is, we look for a typical pattern. The chest motion drops, the body struggles, and then the event ends with a recovery surge: the airway opens, and the person takes one big breath. That pattern is what the radar picks up.":["44d5b0a9cc",16.56,17.92],"In our first year with the engineering team, we tried many methods, and nothing worked. We almost gave up. Then we changed the question. We stopped asking how big each breath is, and started looking for the pattern that ends with one big recovery breath.":["47e8fd4543",14.61,15.96],"After the airway has been blocked for a while, your body suddenly opens it and takes a huge breath, almost like a gasp. That big breath is like a signature. The radar learns to spot it, night after night.":["91e49d10d6",12.57,13.72],"We don't rely on one big breath alone. We look at the whole pattern: the chest motion drops, the effort builds, and then it ends with a recovery surge. It's the sequence that matters, not a single breath.":["90527ec312",12.2,13.5],"We spot each event by its pattern, and the pattern ends with one big recovery breath.":["b3bd31bebe",5.31,5.78],"The radar has to spot a typical pattern of chest-motion changes.":["bc019b56c1",4.22,4.46],"The pattern ends with the recovery surge.":["1aa9e3b34f",2.45,2.58],"When an event ends, the airway opens, and the person takes one big breath.":["50a3daf930",4.46,4.65],"In our first year, nothing worked and we almost gave up.":["8c555fff6b",3.22,3.54],"recovery surge":["01d34fab89",1.28,1.32],"typical":["0b5e9ab957",1.0,0.96],"episode":["ec3781968a",1.05,1.13],"breath, breathe":["92750fccbd",1.28,1.28],"The way out was this:":["4c51b3a6ac",1.43,1.62],"Instead of":["9a9a97ca85",1.0,1.17],"In the end":["2ebb0544e1",0.9,1.09],"What we look for is":["7fa7740e43",1.32,1.47],"How do you know a big breath means an apnea, and not just a deep sigh?":["79065431dd",3.95,4.61],"we almost gave up":["536920a9f1",1.37,1.49],"take a big breath":["23b9c923c1",1.32,1.45],"after the event ends":["7a76072074",1.45,1.69],"In Study 1, we ran the radar side by side with full PSG in 196 patients in the sleep lab. The correlation was strong, r = 0.91. For moderate-to-severe OSA, the accuracy was 90.3%, and for severe OSA, it was 92.4%.":["37840ba4ab",20.14,22.04],"First, we tested it in the hospital, where everything is controlled. 196 patients slept with full PSG and the radar at the same time. The two results lined up very well, with a correlation of 0.91.":["86035a49b4",14.61,15.96],"We asked almost two hundred patients to do the standard hospital sleep test and the radar at the same time, on the same night. The radar's results matched the standard test very closely.":["2154880d62",11.26,12.33],"That's a fair point. Correlation isn't everything, so we also looked at accuracy for severity. For moderate-to-severe OSA it was 90.3%, and for severe OSA 92.4%. And that's why we use it as a screen, not as a replacement for PSG.":["f1f8fd13df",18.26,19.99],"Side by side with PSG in 196 patients, the radar correlated at r = 0.91.":["3ab2b46565",7.4,8.38],"We compared the radar side by side with PSG in the hospital.":["2f3f238563",4.14,4.52],"We tested 196 patients.":["7b61550915",2.37,2.84],"The correlation was strong: r = 0.91.":["39d2443fca",3.39,3.8],"Accuracy was 90.3% for moderate-to-severe OSA and 92.4% for severe OSA.":["393226842a",8.02,8.68],"correlation":["3ad312362d",1.13,1.28],"r = 0.91":["e9f5cefd9b",1.77,2.09],"moderate-to-severe":["041c7502d0",1.24,1.39],"accuracy":["5d6db9a1dc",1.07,1.28],"side by side":["41159e627c",1.32,1.49],"Let's start with Study 1.":["cf2bf82655",1.64,2.11],"Side by side":["5992d5e58a",1.32,1.49],"The correlation was":["626babb51e",1.41,1.54],"Beyond the correlation":["bcfd02731d",1.45,1.56],"In short":["0c728bc435",0.96,1.13],"A correlation of 0.91 can still hide big errors. Is that enough?":["e9caab624a",4.59,4.95],"196 patients":["130470c74a",1.79,2.13],"a strong correlation":["c041504acb",1.47,1.64],"The accuracy was 92.4%.":["1b62d3d171",2.82,3.18],"Then we took it home. Real homes are messy: different sleep positions, thick blankets, noise, and no technician. We tested 80 patients over 147 nights, with no staff on site. Compared with home sleep apnea testing, the correlation was still strong, rho = 0.87.":["b287ecab7b",18.65,19.82],"Study 2 was 80 patients and 147 nights, unsupervised, compared with HSAT. Spearman's rho was 0.87, with p < 0.01. On average, the radar read about 5 events per hour lower. For severe OSA, the accuracy was about 93%.":["633e97690f",19.84,21.55],"You don't have to come to the hospital. We bring the device to your home, you sleep in your own bed, and nobody needs to be there. In our study, the results at home were still close to the standard home sleep test.":["9d2952081b",12.01,13.46],"That was exactly our worry. At home we have blankets, different positions, noise, and nobody watching. So we needed robust signal processing. Even so, the correlation with HSAT was rho = 0.87, and on average it was only about 5 events per hour lower.":["077971e49a",17.66,19.26],"At home, with no staff on site, it still tracked HSAT closely, with rho = 0.87.":["95ca26a272",6.4,6.96],"At home, nothing is controlled: positions, blankets, noise.":["1b27b78d37",3.63,4.08],"There is no technician and no staff on site.":["414922daee",2.8,3.16],"80 patients, 147 nights.":["16f6a8006e",2.75,3.18],"Compared with HSAT, the Spearman correlation was rho = 0.87.":["17aa0b7c79",5.1,5.46],"On average, it was about 5 events per hour lower.":["32ca487905",3.12,3.56],"Spearman":["d7bf0d066b",1.02,1.02],"rho":["843a28dfdc",0.83,0.81],"unsupervised":["f54f5d2e0e",1.2,1.54],"robust":["00bd624b5b",1.02,1.02],"HSAT":["c39768d31b",1.05,1.11],"Now, let's leave the hospital.":["38c569f923",1.86,2.11],"At home, nothing is controlled:":["1ba0f5db70",1.92,2.13],"Even so":["7a799f65e3",0.88,1.0],"On average":["fd62a40f38",1.07,1.28],"Still":["cddbd3031f",0.9,0.88],"Homes are noisy and messy. How can it still work there?":["45ece2cde2",3.29,3.58],"147 nights":["ccebe1bd08",1.77,2.09],"with no one supervising":["5c2606fd6e",1.69,1.75],"no staff on site":["56c64bb897",1.45,1.62],"The device is TFDA cleared, but only as a screen. If the bRDI is 30 or more, we flag the patient as suspected severe OSA. At that cut-off, sensitivity and specificity are both 90.5%. It does not diagnose OSA on its own.":["718dfe0154",18.62,20.69],"Think of it like a smoke alarm. It tells us when something might be wrong, so we know who needs a full sleep test first. But it doesn't give you the final diagnosis. Your doctor still confirms it with a standard test.":["fab378dbdc",12.76,14.32],"If the morning report shows a bRDI of 30 or more, that's a flag for suspected severe OSA. It's a screen, not a diagnosis, so the next step is to tell the doctor, and the doctor decides on a full sleep test.":["ee9724bec6",14.55,15.74],"No. TFDA cleared it as a screen for suspected severe OSA, not as a diagnostic test. It helps us find who needs further testing, with 90.5% sensitivity and specificity at a cut-off of 30. The diagnosis still needs a standard sleep test.":["94d751aa47",18.16,19.48],"It's a TFDA-cleared screen for suspected severe OSA, not a diagnosis.":["ea6634d760",5.44,6.25],"The device is TFDA cleared.":["c9eb2d6553",2.11,2.18],"It is used only as a screen for suspected severe OSA.":["f0cfcb5b74",3.82,4.27],"The cut-off is a bRDI of 30 or more.":["0fec9d8648",3.01,3.22],"At that cut-off, sensitivity and specificity are both 90.5%.":["557778952a",5.12,5.72],"It does not diagnose OSA on its own.":["b36ae474b9",2.6,3.12],"clearance":["9a2f834680",1.05,1.0],"screen":["599eba19aa",1.0,1.0],"suspected":["d73d9db45f",1.09,1.15],"sensitivity":["c742484dfb",1.11,1.34],"specificity":["86039b0dda",1.17,1.3],"Please note:":["46889411c1",1.07,1.17],"To be clear":["e8044a4de3",1.07,1.2],"What it does is":["920210e761",1.13,1.3],"So the next step is":["67a9722df0",1.39,1.66],"So can I use this instead of PSG to diagnose OSA?":["a6f241c12e",4.12,4.57],"It's TFDA cleared as a screen for suspected severe OSA.":["e3a552b06d",4.27,4.74],"At a cut-off of 30":["6a53535592",1.47,1.84],"sensitivity and specificity are both 90.5%":["64d4e17c51",4.1,4.57],"Once it's installed, it runs around the clock. You'll see every patient on one dashboard: status, movement, and any abnormal vital signs. If a value is abnormal or a patient gets out of bed, you get a LINE alert right away.":["4552404985",14.53,16.19],"The radar sends data nonstop over Wi-Fi. A dashboard shows all the patients in one place, and the system sends automatic LINE alerts for abnormal values or bed exits. Every morning, it also makes a report from the night before.":["fa7cae86df",14.78,16.26],"In practice, the radar streams data over Wi-Fi around the clock. The dashboard shows each bed's status, and staff get LINE alerts for abnormal values or bed exits. Every morning, the team also gets a report for each bed: bRDI, sleep time, PB percentage, and the average PB cycle length.":["dd30a1b7c9",19.9,21.93],"While you sleep, nobody needs to come in and check wires. The device quietly watches your breathing, and if something looks wrong, the nurses get a message on their phones right away.":["c2d5e1604e",10.41,11.5],"The alerts are only for abnormal values or bed exits, not for every breath. And instead of walking from bed to bed, the staff can see everyone on one dashboard. So the idea is to focus their attention, not to add more work.":["bc5f9b38dc",14.46,15.96],"It runs around the clock, shows everyone on one dashboard, and sends LINE alerts when something is wrong.":["95a2b1fe6f",6.27,6.98],"The radar sends data nonstop over Wi-Fi.":["35e1d41d7a",2.92,3.16],"The dashboard shows each patient in one place.":["93a385edf0",2.54,2.9],"Staff get an automatic LINE alert for abnormal values or bed exits.":["2ec78973d3",4.52,4.93],"Every morning, the radar makes a report from the night before.":["9c0a4bd004",3.52,3.9],"dashboard":["dc7161be3d",0.77,0.88],"alert":["7ed2114307",0.9,0.94],"vital signs":["ce1c4256c1",1.2,1.39],"bed exit":["8b48608465",1.15,1.15],"around the clock":["576ca5502f",1.26,1.43],"Once it's installed":["db65b60c21",1.28,1.6],"Right away":["dcf2b2387e",0.88,1.05],"Every morning":["42773bd470",1.13,1.28],"So in practice":["17fa2f70c7",1.2,1.45],"Won't all these alerts just add to the nurses' workload?":["d8938db702",2.99,3.35],"the staff":["1015ad8150",0.98,0.92],"when a patient gets out of bed":["89a6020ce5",1.52,1.69],"the nurses get a LINE alert":["5eb22af8f2",1.81,1.92],"Periodic breathing means the breathing grows and fades. There's a phase of heavy breathing, then an apnea, again and again. One heavy phase plus one apnea is one cycle, and the time it takes is the cycle length. It's often seen in heart failure.":["dae5c0d5a0",16.11,17.37],"Imagine waves on a beach: a big wave, then calm, then another big wave. Some people breathe like that in their sleep, strong breaths, then a pause, over and over. We often see this in people with heart problems.":["3a68e14cbd",12.89,14.4],"On the raw radar signal, you can see the breathing grow and fade, over and over. Our algorithm detects each cycle, so the cycle length is measured automatically, night after night, from a continuous signal. And periodic breathing is often seen in heart failure.":["a1aeee0765",16.34,17.88],"There's some overlap, because each cycle includes an apnea. But the pattern is different: the breathing grows and fades in a smooth cycle. That pattern, also called Cheyne-Stokes breathing, is often seen in heart failure, so it may tell us something about the heart.":["48af44a116",15.77,16.96],"Periodic breathing grows and fades in cycles, and the cycle length may tell us something about the heart.":["b919a7c087",5.93,6.66],"In periodic breathing, the breathing grows and fades.":["2c58c6fafe",3.31,3.67],"A phase of heavy breathing, then an apnea, again and again.":["049295954b",3.5,4.05],"The time one cycle takes is the PB cycle length.":["4fa2157f3a",3.29,3.76],"Periodic breathing is often seen in heart failure.":["9ce1f8d0fc",3.29,3.54],"From the radar signal, we can measure the cycle length automatically, night after night.":["55073bad74",5.08,5.55],"periodic":["e6ffdec5e1",1.2,1.17],"Cheyne-Stokes":["d7d7be35ac",1.13,1.24],"hyperpnea":["160dbbe58e",1.2,1.45],"cycle length":["1b0ede9919",1.2,1.28],"heart failure":["741b702150",1.09,1.32],"So what exactly is":["d5c746faa7",1.45,1.66],"Look at":["f39cc1956f",0.92,0.9],"Again and again":["ac82ca3a8c",1.26,1.58],"Isn't periodic breathing just another kind of sleep apnea?":["cf4702733c",3.54,3.97],"the breathing grows and fades":["43833765a6",1.77,1.92],"the cycle length":["b32dea2da4",1.3,1.37],"chain-STOKES":["763282172a",1.13,1.24],"Our breathing is run by a feedback loop, and high loop gain means the loop overreacts. There are two main reasons. In OSA, the airway keeps closing and opening. In heart failure, blood moves slowly, so the signals arrive late, and the brain is always one step behind: too much, then too little.":["638b83e860",18.24,19.99],"It's like a shower where the water takes a long time to change temperature. You turn it hot, nothing happens, so you turn it more. Then it's way too hot, so you turn it cold. When the heart is weak, the brain controls breathing the same way: always a little late, too much, then too little.":["3d1686f201",16.47,18.03],"Think of a control system with a long delay. When the heart is weak, circulation is slow, so the sensors that control breathing get the CO2 signal late. The controller overcorrects, and the system swings. That swing is what we see as periodic breathing.":["82375b6b33",16.26,17.64],"No. Just to be clear, our radar does not measure CO2 or oxygen. It only sees the breathing pattern. The loop-gain idea explains that pattern: when the heart is weak, the signals arrive late, so the breathing loop overreacts. The radar measures the cycle length of those swings.":["cb246d9f70",17.66,19.73],"When the heart is weak, the breathing control loop reacts too late and too much, so breathing swings.":["ef08828dfd",5.33,6.25],"Our breathing is run by a feedback loop.":["c47c130200",2.39,2.65],"High loop gain means the loop overreacts.":["3a51272473",2.52,2.86],"First reason: airway resistance, like in OSA.":["166494a4e4",3.35,3.69],"Second: in heart failure, blood moves slowly, so signals arrive late.":["00f26c8809",4.37,4.46],"Our radar does not measure CO2 or oxygen; it only sees the breathing pattern.":["671aa3067d",5.25,5.93],"loop gain":["a3630d7b77",1.11,1.2],"feedback loop":["9c7caa5a70",1.09,1.32],"overreact":["bd1a4c4667",1.07,1.2],"carbon dioxide":["e4535db666",1.39,1.56],"one step behind":["e172c3d28e",1.32,1.58],"Here's the idea:":["1c8dc1e9b1",1.24,1.47],"Think of it like":["7cb6bfe34a",1.17,1.3],"There are two main reasons.":["7ed96f80c8",1.54,1.71],"First, Second":["453d634c32",1.24,1.32],"Just to be clear":["194c21858e",1.26,1.32],"Does the radar measure CO2 to show this?":["d0c20afd5f",2.67,3.03],"The brain is always one step behind.":["dbddcfd373",2.3,2.6],"too much, then too little":["fda714749b",1.52,1.73],"blood moves more slowly":["3f68952d27",1.54,1.64],"In Study 3, with 72 patients, we asked whether breathing can tell us about the heart. When the radar picked up periodic breathing, it was linked to a lower LVEF. Just to be clear, it's an association. It isn't validated yet for diagnosis or prediction.":["3189bf7e28",17.34,18.6],"In our 2025 paper, a PB cycle one second longer was associated with NT-proBNP 158.49 picograms per milliliter higher, and LVEF 0.21% lower. These are clinical associations only, not a validated predictor.":["6975c3e00d",18.94,20.97],"We put the radar in the cardiology ward, and we really saw a lot of Cheyne-Stokes breathing. So we asked: can the breathing pattern tell us how well the heart pumps? We found that periodic breathing was associated with a lower LVEF. It's an early-warning idea, not a diagnosis.":["2c20e2b001",17.62,19.73],"Your breathing at night may give us clues about your heart. In our study, people whose breathing went up and down in cycles tended to have weaker heart pumping. But for now it's only a clue, not a test for heart failure.":["b096c7a839",13.04,13.91],"No, not yet. What we found are associations: periodic breathing was linked to a lower LVEF and a higher NT-proBNP. That makes it an interesting early-warning idea, but it isn't validated for diagnosis or prediction.":["329f1f65a1",16.15,17.6],"Periodic breathing on the radar is linked to weaker heart function, but it's an association, not a diagnosis.":["1ff315c04d",7.06,7.51],"Study 3 had 72 patients.":["1ae82f486f",2.28,2.33],"Periodic breathing was linked to a lower LVEF.":["2febfe9019",3.52,3.86],"These are associations only.":["4816c2da47",1.84,2.18],"It isn't validated yet for diagnosis or prediction.":["55c5643f50",3.65,3.95],"If the PB cycle is one second longer, NT-proBNP goes up 158.49 pg/mL.":["a4b605794e",8.77,9.92],"association":["af14b067a4",1.2,1.32],"LVEF":["a3081f902c",1.26,1.45],"ejection fraction":["6d838f6514",1.39,1.47],"NT-proBNP":["10f3598a33",1.6,1.98],"validated":["c9e825f464",1.13,1.17],"Now let's turn to the heart.":["16fc9a20af",1.62,1.9],"Here's what we found:":["86058ce2ef",1.32,1.54],"That's a big change, for just one second.":["00c506e0d8",2.54,2.77],"It's an association, not a diagnosis.":["d4a552c37f",2.69,3.03],"Are you saying the radar can predict heart failure?":["db2e469425",2.86,3.09],"is associated with a lower LVEF":["8dab09c0fa",2.56,2.86],"It's linked to lower LVEF, but it isn't validated for prediction.":["5d7aade1e7",4.4,4.97],"one second longer":["679f61bec8",1.39,1.69],"To wrap up, four points. It's contactless, so we can monitor breathing night after night. It agrees with standard tests: r = 0.91 in the hospital and rho = 0.87 at home. It's a TFDA-cleared screen for suspected severe OSA. And periodic breathing is linked to the heart.":["802deb076d",19.99,21.18],"In short: a small device can watch your breathing every night without touching you. It matches the standard tests well, it can tell us who may have severe sleep apnea, and it may even give us clues about the heart.":["632c5f1975",11.99,13.7],"Three numbers to remember: r = 0.91 against PSG, rho = 0.87 against HSAT at home, and 90.5% sensitivity as a TFDA-cleared screen for suspected severe OSA. Plus an association between periodic breathing and cardiac impairment.":["98dbd2928d",20.2,21.8],"It's contactless, so patients wear nothing, and we can monitor every night instead of one night in a lab. It's consistent with PSG and home testing, and it's a TFDA-cleared screen for suspected severe OSA. And it may even help us watch the heart.":["3fb84bc9cf",16.88,18.09],"Nothing to wear, consistent with standard tests, a TFDA-cleared screen for severe OSA, and a possible window on the heart.":["c1185d623f",8.58,9.71],"It's contactless and can monitor breathing night after night.":["ab6f364114",3.24,3.82],"It's consistent with standard tests: r = 0.91 in the hospital and rho = 0.87 at home.":["cd45263063",7.3,7.79],"It's a TFDA-cleared screen for suspected severe OSA.":["f49787ca20",4.12,4.72],"Periodic breathing is associated with cardiac impairment.":["a2eca21343",3.76,4.16],"take-home message":["235e8f76c3",1.37,1.49],"contactless":["3d4546e9f0",1.15,1.26],"consistent":["3a6713def8",1.07,1.17],"cardiac impairment":["79ea1124fc",1.34,1.54],"To wrap up":["7c349601e6",1.07,1.17],"First, Second, Third, And finally":["79b080df41",2.35,2.45],"So it may help us":["ef03b51d58",1.28,1.54],"Thank you very much.":["9d197f9a18",1.41,1.54],"I'm happy to take your questions.":["9e7e83a60c",1.92,2.24],"If you had 30 seconds to convince a hospital director, what would you say?":["419579a11e",4.14,4.95],"Thank you for listening.":["ee26d140bc",1.43,1.6],"It may help us watch the heart, too.":["fb953f1fb1",2.01,2.43],"Good question. There are a few. First, it's a screen, not a diagnosis. Second, the radar doesn't measure oxygen or CO2. It only sees the breathing pattern. Third, at home it read about 5 events per hour lower on average. And the heart findings are associations only.":["449bc22b01",18.2,19.63],"To be honest, it doesn't do everything. It doesn't measure oxygen, and it doesn't diagnose OSA on its own. Its job is to screen for suspected severe OSA and to watch breathing night after night. When the screen is positive, we still do a standard sleep test.":["ade92b5123",16.98,18.52],"Four caveats. One, the TFDA clearance is for screening suspected severe OSA, at a bRDI of 30 or more, so a positive screen still needs a standard sleep test. Two, the radar doesn't measure oxygen or CO2. Three, at home it read about 5 events per hour lower than HSAT on average. Four, the cardiac findings are associations, not validated for prediction.":["22b2389810",24.94,27.5],"This device is like a first check. It can tell us if you might have severe sleep apnea, but it doesn't check your oxygen level, and it can't give the final answer. If it finds a problem, we'll still do a full sleep test.":["2ead3b4519",12.99,13.97],"It's a screen that sees breathing patterns only, not oxygen, so a positive result still needs a standard test.":["6e0a104eb3",6.93,7.74],"It's a screen, not a diagnosis.":["ff88a91fa1",2.2,2.54],"It doesn't measure oxygen or CO2.":["8cae897102",2.62,2.9],"At home it read about 5 events per hour lower on average.":["245f9cc871",3.5,3.93],"The heart findings are associations only.":["b827c6356d",2.65,2.97],"When the screen is positive, we still do a standard sleep test.":["98bfdfb90e",3.56,3.95],"limitation":["d8595af350",1.09,1.3],"underestimate":["a5d26aacaa",1.13,1.34],"caveat":["9949de969e",0.98,1.07],"oxygen":["6124d98749",1.02,1.17],"That's a great question.":["c246314e65",1.47,1.71],"To be honest":["fd24d77609",1.13,1.28],"There are a few limits.":["bce8df1788",1.32,1.58],"First, Also":["321a353f9c",1.26,1.34],"So we still need":["40428e7fa2",1.28,1.49],"What are the main limitations of your radar?":["6e88fbc9d4",2.39,2.84],"It doesn't measure oxygen.":["3fddbd4235",1.66,1.98],"One limit is that it":["d8c23981ce",1.41,1.52],"It doesn't replace PSG.":["cb49231db4",1.86,2.09],"The big difference is simple: wearables have to be worn, and the radar doesn't. Watches, rings, and patches all sit on the body. They can be uncomfortable, and patches can cause allergy. With radar, there's nothing to wear, so we can monitor night after night.":["c0960c7c1f",15.81,17.28],"With a watch or a patch, you have to remember to put it on, and some people get a skin rash from the patch. With this device, you just go to sleep. Nothing touches you, so it's easy to use every night.":["338c4b1e64",11.95,13.25],"Type I to Type IV tests and consumer wearables all need sensors on the body. That limits comfort and long-term use. A contactless radar removes that barrier, so we can follow breathing night after night, not just for one or two nights.":["82c40dc875",15.13,16.88],"Wearables are great for many things. But every one of them has to be worn, and during sleep that matters: they can be uncomfortable, and people take them off. The radar needs nothing on the body, so we can monitor night after night.":["4bb12f538b",13.48,14.81],"Wearables must be worn; the radar needs nothing on the body, so it works night after night.":["486ecea672",5.38,6.14],"Every wearable has to be worn on the body.":["b3e1657a18",2.58,2.94],"They are uncomfortable, and the patch can cause allergy.":["99f61e2e99",3.12,3.56],"With radar, there is nothing to wear.":["4723d8bfe0",2.22,2.48],"So we can monitor night after night.":["d5704369a0",1.98,2.39],"wearable":["27c316cf67",0.96,1.02],"non-wearable":["91231d4b6b",1.09,1.34],"allergy":["e13d519f1d",1.05,1.15],"uncomfortable":["c19a5c3628",1.05,1.24],"night after night":["84d2ce226c",1.24,1.47],"The big difference is":["62bd6d923d",1.37,1.49],"Unlike wearables":["1f20c18d57",1.28,1.58],"With wearables, ; with radar":["b7a94ce7d2",1.73,1.88],"That means":["0553c37931",0.98,1.05],"Especially for":["edf7b48fbc",1.15,1.3],"Wearables are cheap and everywhere. Why bother with radar?":["ba57d92af1",3.46,3.9],"It has to be worn on the body.":["bad35da945",1.88,2.07],"comfortable for patients":["39ffe0e3b4",1.54,1.58],"That's an important question. The radar is not a camera. It doesn't take pictures of the patient. What it picks up is chest motion, which we turn into a breathing signal. So what we see is a breathing wave, not a person.":["6fa866e7bb",12.99,14.06],"Many people hear 'radar' and think of a camera. It isn't. It only senses movement, mainly the chest rising and falling, and turns that into a breathing signal. There are no pictures of your face or your body.":["6502688775",13.04,13.7],"That's a fair question, but I don't have the exact cost numbers with me today. What I can say is that there's nothing to wear, and at home no technician has to be on site. I'd be happy to follow up after the session.":["72b7fd7d86",12.67,13.68],"Don't worry, it's not a camera. It can't see your face. It only notices your chest moving up and down while you sleep.":["7b98377dbe",6.74,7.57],"It's not a camera: it only turns chest motion into a breathing signal.":["56e9621cb8",4.18,4.69],"The radar is not a camera. It doesn't take pictures.":["ca132bd1d2",3.33,3.56],"It only picks up chest motion and turns it into a breathing signal.":["89e632c42b",3.73,4.31],"I don't have the exact numbers today, but I'd be happy to follow up.":["09adebbc77",3.67,4.2],"privacy":["21cec257fe",1.11,1.13],"camera":["dd6d2dcc67",0.92,1.02],"follow up":["ea50dd49e3",1.11,1.17],"That's an important question.":["b7cea67081",1.69,1.92],"The short answer is":["da3ee002ea",1.37,1.47],"What it picks up is":["a08aefc3ce",1.24,1.45],"I don't have the exact numbers with me today, but":["b038ab0680",2.71,3.07],"I'd be happy to follow up after the session.":["de660fd4eb",2.35,2.8],"Is there a privacy problem with a radar in the bedroom?":["a79ab656fd",3.26,3.54],"How much does the system cost?":["336bf70a3e",1.81,2.24],"It doesn't take pictures.":["c362fe077f",1.58,1.69],"I don't have that number with me today.":["3086bc6b5b",2.01,2.45],"The radar senses chest motion, not images.":["1ee6bf0a79",2.97,3.37],"Explain it simply, in about 30 seconds.":["ed73f23dde",2.58,3.03],"Explain it to a patient or a family member. Use an everyday comparison.":["0dba8ed882",4.63,5.14],"Explain it to a ward nurse. What does it mean for daily care?":["1385fc7d42",3.56,4.22],"Explain it to an engineer. Focus on how it works.":["df8e9d549b",3.16,3.69],"Explain it to another doctor. Lead with the data.":["48b7332341",2.99,3.41],"Someone in the audience challenges you. Answer the question.":["2b1bcc1b49",3.39,3.9],"Start with a story or an experience, then make the point.":["53a40e0152",3.31,3.76],"You don't have the exact answer. Answer honestly and helpfully.":["c9061adcff",3.63,4.2],"Say it in one sentence.":["59b15b09ab",1.56,1.77]}